module gunbc.runner.runner_guest_egress_attempt

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr, String }

// AN ATTEMPT THAT DID NOT REACH ITS GOAL, recorded because two of its by-products are worth more
// than the goal was. Attempt slice7c-bridge-e6a5b5c2 on the Mt. Collins unit, 2026-09-07. The aim
// was guest egress -- a runner cannot register with GitHub over a /30 to its host -- by bridging the
// attempt's tap and the host NIC onto the LAN. The guest did not obtain egress.
//
// IT IS ITS OWN RECEIPT RATHER THAN A FOOTNOTE ON A SUCCESS, because a reader looking for "does the
// guest have egress" must find the answer no, and because the two findings below were produced by
// this run and would otherwise be attributed to whichever later run happens to succeed.
data egress_attempt: NonEmptyStr = "slice7c-bridge-e6a5b5c2"
data egress_attempt_evidence: NonEmptyStr = "artifacts/bmc/mtcollins1-bridge-egress-attempt.log"
data egress_attempt_evidence_sha256: NonEmptyStr = "694f5a64bcd8f3739091f28a0b1f4bdfde94e0c144d0bf9c6efa43e87bf688a9"
data egress_attempt_evidence_byte_count: Int = 225444

type EgressAttemptOutcome
  = GuestEgressEstablished { via: NonEmptyStr }
  | GuestEgressNotEstablished { reached: NonEmptyStr, blocked_at: NonEmptyStr }

data egress_outcome: EgressAttemptOutcome = GuestEgressNotEstablished {
  reached: "the bridge carries eth0 and the attempt tap, and the host NIC reports link up at 1000 Mbps full duplex",
  blocked_at: "the host obtained no address on the bridge, so neither host nor guest had a route off the segment",
}

// ── the NIC actually carries a link ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// WHAT WAS OBSERVED IS LINK NEGOTIATION, AND AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS ROW CLAIMED MORE. The line
//
//   igb 0002:02:00.0 eth0: igb: eth0 NIC Link is Up 1000 Mbps Full Duplex, Flow Control: RX/TX
//
// establishes that the driver bound and the PHY negotiated 1000 Mbps full duplex on the port whose
// MAC the firmware used for PXE. It does NOT establish that the interface carried a single frame.
//
// SO THE HARDWARE LANE'S OPEN ITEM STAYS OPEN. runner_host_hardware_observation lists "that either
// interface carries traffic" among the things a PCI identity cannot settle, and this row was written
// as though it closed that. It does not: closing it needs packet counters moving or a capture, on
// this boot. Carrier is not a data plane, and promoting one to the other is how a layer-1 reading
// becomes a reachability claim.
type LinkObservation
  = PhysicalLinkNegotiated { interface: NonEmptyStr, speed: NonEmptyStr, duplex: NonEmptyStr }
  | InterfaceCarriedTraffic { evidence: NonEmptyStr }

data nic_link_observed: LinkObservation = PhysicalLinkNegotiated {
  interface: "eth0, the fleet-facing port",
  speed: "1000 Mbps",
  duplex: "full",
}

// ── the bridge was correct, and only its readback was broken ────────────────────────────────────

// AN EARLIER RUN REPORTED AN EMPTY BRIDGE AND THE BRIDGE WAS FINE. Reading
// /sys/class/net/br0/brif shows both interfaces enslaved.
//
// THE EXPLANATION FIRST GIVEN FOR THE EMPTY OUTPUT WAS WRONG AND IS WITHDRAWN. This row claimed
// `brctl show br0` takes no argument in busybox; busybox 1.36.1 documents `show [BRIDGE]...` and
// iterates the bridges it is given, so the invocation was valid and the explanation was invented to
// fit the symptom. WHY that rendering came back empty is unresolved.
//
// REPLACING A BAD MEASUREMENT WITH A CONFIDENT WRONG CAUSE IS WORSE THAN LEAVING IT OPEN, because
// the correction reads as understanding and stops anyone looking further. What survives is narrow:
// sysfs established membership, the brctl rendering did not agree with it, and the disagreement is
// unexplained.
data bridge_membership: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "eth0 -- the host NIC",
  "the attempt's tap device",
]

data bridge_readback_defect: NonEmptyStr = "sysfs established both bridge members; an earlier brctl show br0 rendering came back empty and the cause is unresolved -- the first explanation offered, that busybox rejects the argument, is false and is withdrawn"

// ── the cpu bound has a cause now ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE PREVIOUS RECEIPT SAID NO CAUSE IS CLAIMED, and this run produced one. Writing cpu.max fails
// with a located error rather than silently doing nothing:
//
//   /init: can't create /sys/fs/cgroup/gunbc/attempt-<id>/cpu.max: Permission denied
//
// WITH THE ROOT'S SUBTREE CARRYING cpu. cgroup-root-subtree reads "cpu memory pids", so the
// controller is delegated from the root -- and the interface file still cannot be created in the
// leaf. memory.max and pids.max in the same leaf apply.
//
// AND THE CAUSE IS NOW READ FROM THE KERNEL CONFIG RATHER THAN GUESSED: the built config carries
//
//   CONFIG_CGROUP_SCHED=y
//   CONFIG_FAIR_GROUP_SCHED=y
//   # CONFIG_CFS_BANDWIDTH is not set
//
// CFS bandwidth provisioning is what creates cpu.max on a non-root cgroup, and it defaults off. So
// the file does not exist, the shell redirect tried to CREATE an arbitrary file inside cgroupfs, and
// the kernel answered EPERM. The cpu controller being listed in cgroup.controllers is consistent
// with this: weight-based control is present and bandwidth control is not.
//
// THE REPAIR IS A KERNEL OPTION, and the acceptance is not "cpu.max reads back". A present interface
// is not an enforced boundary -- the receipt for that owes a controlled CPU load and cpu.stat
// throttling counters moving.
type CpuBoundFailure
  = CpuBoundAppliedNow { value: NonEmptyStr }
  | CpuBoundRefusedWithErrno { errno: NonEmptyStr, path_shape: NonEmptyStr, root_subtree: NonEmptyStr }

data cpu_bound_failure: CpuBoundFailure = CpuBoundRefusedWithErrno {
  errno: "EPERM -- the file does not exist and cgroupfs refuses its creation, because CONFIG_CFS_BANDWIDTH is not set in this kernel",
  path_shape: "the attempt leaf's cpu.max",
  root_subtree: "cpu memory pids -- the controller is delegated, and weight control is not bandwidth control",
}

// ── what stopped the egress, at the grain observed ──────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE DHCP CLIENT REPORTED SUCCESS AND APPLIED NOTHING, TWICE, FOR TWO DIFFERENT REASONS -- and the
// second is unresolved.
//
// FIRST: this image's callback applied no configuration and udhcpc exited 0 with no address, which
// reads exactly like a working client. A -s script was added.
//
// SECOND, AND OPEN: the script is present at a known path and its log is empty. An earlier revision
// concluded from that "udhcpc never invoked it" and called the facts irreconcilable. They are not.
// The empty log establishes only that the script BODY never reached its first statement -- and
// udhcpc does not inspect or propagate a callback's result, so an exec that failed before the log
// (mode, interpreter line, mount flags, path) is indistinguishable from a callback never attempted.
//
// SO THE HONEST STANDING IS THAT THE CALLBACK PATH IS UNPROVEN AT BOTH ENDS. Candidate causes are
// enumerable -- script not executable, noexec mount, missing or malformed interpreter, log
// destination unwritable, the -s argument not reaching the applet -- and none is excluded.
//
// AND A THIRD POSSIBILITY REMAINS OPEN TOO: that the segment answered no DHCP for the bridge's MAC.
// Nothing distinguishes "no offer" from "offer obtained and discarded", which is exactly what the
// lease log was added to settle and did not.
data egress_blocked_by: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "this image's callback did not apply configuration and udhcpc exited 0 with no address, which is indistinguishable from a working client. An earlier revision said busybox applies nothing WITHOUT -s; that is too strong, since busybox carries a compiled default script path and -s replaces it",
  "with a script supplied and present in the image, its log is empty -- which establishes the script body did not reach its first statement, NOT that udhcpc never attempted the callback. udhcpc does not propagate a callback failure, so an exec that failed before the log would look exactly like this",
  "and no observation distinguishes 'the segment offered no lease' from 'a lease was obtained and discarded'",
]

// ── the policy debt this incurred ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// BRIDGING IS WIDER THAN THE ISOLATION PROFILE ALLOWS AND WAS CHOSEN ANYWAY. runner_isolation_admission
// requires DefaultDenyEgress for every untrusted class; a bridge gives a guest unfiltered access to
// the whole segment. It is admissible for RunnerHostQualificationProbe running one digest-pinned
// image and is NOT admissible for any real job.
//
// THE REASON IT WAS CHOSEN IS A CAPABILITY GAP, NOT A PREFERENCE. This image has no netfilter
// userspace at all -- busybox carries brctl and ip and neither nft nor iptables -- and CONFIG_MODULES=n
// means anything the kernel defconfig marks modular is absent outright. A filtered egress path needs
// its own kernel options and its own binary, which is its own slice.
data egress_policy_debt: NonEmptyStr = "the bridge grants unfiltered segment access, which DefaultDenyEgress forbids; admissible only for the qualification profile with one pinned image, and a prerequisite for any real job"

data egress_open: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "why udhcpc exits 0 without invoking its own script, and whether the segment offered a lease at all",
  "a kernel built with CONFIG_CFS_BANDWIDTH, and then a controlled load showing cpu.stat throttling -- the interface existing would not establish enforcement",
  "a filtered egress path: netfilter built in, an nft or equivalent binary in the image, and a default-deny ruleset",
  "guest egress itself, which nothing here established",
  "the JIT-registered runner, which egress is the prerequisite for",
]
